% \clearpage
\section{Results}

% Paragraph
% --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- ---
Our dataset comprised 11 deciduous tree species with leaf phenology data spanning nine years (2016 to 2024) for a total of 
\Sexpr{nrow(empfullsosts)} leafout and 
\Sexpr{nrow(empfulleosts)} leaf coloring
observations, yielding 
\Sexpr{nrow(empts)} complete growing seasons for 
\Sexpr{length(unique(empts$id))} individuals. Over the duration of the study, mean summer temperatures spanned 
\Sexpr{tsjja$tmeanmean[which.min(tsjja$tmeanmean)]}$^\circ$C 
(\Sexpr{tsjja$year[which.min(tsjja$tmeanmean)]}) to 
\Sexpr{tsjja$tmeanmean[which.max(tsjja$tmeanmean)]}$^\circ$C 
(\Sexpr{tsjja$year[which.max(tsjja$tmeanmean)]}), with an average of
\Sexpr{mean(tsjja$tmeanmean)}$^\circ$C. 2020 and 2022 had moderate droughts in the summer (June, July and August), which ramped up to severe in September. Additionally, a moderate drought in June 2016 ramped up to severe in July, then extreme in August and September. 

The calendar season (GSL) ranged from 
\Sexpr{min(empts$pgsGSL)} days ({\Afla} in 
\Sexpr{empts$year[which.min(empts$pgsGSL)]}) to 
\Sexpr{max(empts$pgsGSL)} days ({\Asac} in 
\Sexpr{empts$year[which.max(empts$pgsGSL)]}), while the thermal season (GDD) ranged from 
\Sexpr{round(min(empts$pgsGDD5),1)} GDD ({\Afla} in 
\Sexpr{empts$year[which.min(empts$pgsGDD5)]}) to 
\Sexpr{round(max(empts$pgsGDD5),1)} GDD ({\Tame} in 
\Sexpr{empts$year[which.max(empts$pgsGDD5)]}). The earliest start of season (leafout) was 
\Sexpr{empts$loCal[which.min(empts$leafout)]} ({\Afla} in 
\Sexpr{empts$year[which.min(empts$leafout)]}) and the latest, 
\Sexpr{empts$loCal[which.max(empts$leafout)]} ({\Cova} in 
\Sexpr{empts$year[which.max(empts$leafout)]}), while the earliest end of season (leaf coloring) day of year was
\Sexpr{empts$clCal[which.min(empts$coloredLeaves)]} ({\Afla} in 
\Sexpr{empts$year[which.min(empts$coloredLeaves)]}---same individual with earliest leafout) and the latest, 
\Sexpr{empts$clCal[which.max(empts$coloredLeaves)]} ({\Tame} in
\Sexpr{empts$year[which.max(empts$coloredLeaves)]}). Annual ring widths across years varied from 
\Sexpr{min(empts$lengthMM)} mm ({\Asac} in \Sexpr{empts$year[which.min(empts$lengthMM)]}) to 
\Sexpr{max(empts$lengthMM)} mm ({\Tame} in \Sexpr{empts$year[which.max(empts$lengthMM)]}; Figure \ref{fig:tsboxplot}). 

% Paragraph
Across our eleven species and season predictors, we did not find any clear response of growing season length on growth. 
We report estimates as means and 90\% uncertainty intervals (UI) that correspond to ring width change in mm on the log scale---henceforth `RW/day' or `RW/GDD' with days and GDD being adjusted to represent more interpretable units (see `Data analyses' section in Methods for more details on predictor adjustment).
A longer thermal season did not increase growth for any species and decreased growth for {\Ball}
($\beta =$ \Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_gdd$mean[bspp_df2_ts_gdd$spp_name %in% "B. alleghaniensis"]}, UI:
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_gdd$p5[bspp_df2_ts_gdd$spp_name %in% "B. alleghaniensis"]},
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_gdd$p95[bspp_df2_ts_gdd$spp_name %in% "B. alleghaniensis"]} RW/GDD; Figure \ref{fig:tsmuplotbspp}a and e; Table \ref{tab:ts_slope_table}). 
Further, a longer calendar season did not change growth for any species (Figure \ref{fig:tsmuplotbspp}b and f; Table \ref{tab:ts_slope_table}). On a scaled axis \citep[through $z$-scoring,][]{gelman_data_2006}, the thermal and calendar seasons were similarly predictive (see Figure \ref{fig:bsppZscored}).

% Paragraph
Previous year' climate also had limited effects on growth. For the one species---{\Ball}---that showed decreased growth with warmer thermal seasons, this effect persisted for the current year, but previous year's GDD actually increased growth 
($\beta =$ \Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_prvs$mean[bspp_df2_ts_prvs$spp_name %in% "B. alleghaniensis"]}, UI:
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_prvs$p5[bspp_df2_ts_prvs$spp_name %in% "B. alleghaniensis"]},
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_prvs$p95[bspp_df2_ts_prvs$spp_name %in% "B. alleghaniensis"]} RW/GDD; Figure \ref{fig:tsPrvsYr}b; Table \ref{tab:tab_PrvsYr}). The congeneric of {\Ball}, {\Bnig}, in contrast grew less with previous year's GDD ($\beta =$ 
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", bspp_df2_ts_prvs$mean[bspp_df2_ts_prvs$spp_name %in% "B. nigra"])}, UI:
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", bspp_df2_ts_prvs$p5[bspp_df2_ts_prvs$spp_name %in% "B. nigra"])},
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", bspp_df2_ts_prvs$p95[bspp_df2_ts_prvs$spp_name %in% "B. nigra"])} RW/GDD; Figure \ref{fig:tsPrvsYr}b; Table \ref{tab:tab_PrvsYr}) and current year GDD increased growth ($\beta =$ 
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_curr$mean[bspp_df2_ts_curr$spp_name %in% "B. nigra"]}, UI:
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_curr$p5[bspp_df2_ts_curr$spp_name %in% "B. nigra"]},
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_curr$p95[bspp_df2_ts_curr$spp_name %in% "B. nigra"]} RW/GDD; Figure \ref{fig:tsPrvsYr}a; Table \ref{tab:tab_PrvsYr}).
Interestingly, {\Bnig} did not solely respond to the current year's conditions (Figure \ref{fig:tsmuplotbspp}a and e; Table \ref{tab:ts_slope_table}), but grew more with warmer thermal seasons only when the previous conditions were considered. 

% Paragraph
An earlier SOS did not increase growth for any species and decreased growth for {\Tame} (
$\beta =$ \Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_sos$mean[bspp_df2_ts_sos$spp_name %in% "T. americana"]}, UI:
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_sos$p5[bspp_df2_ts_sos$spp_name %in% "T. americana"]},
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_sos$p95[bspp_df2_ts_sos$spp_name %in% "T. americana"]} RW/day; Figure \ref{fig:tsmuplotbspp}c and g; Table \ref{tab:ts_slope_table}), which did not respond to the thermal or the calendar season. Additionally, a later end of season did not increase growth for any species and decreased growth for {\Ball}
($\beta =$ \Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_eos$mean[bspp_df2_ts_eos$spp_name %in% "B. alleghaniensis"]}, UI:
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_eos$p5[bspp_df2_ts_eos$spp_name %in% "B. alleghaniensis"]},
\Sexpr{bspp_df2_ts_eos$p95[bspp_df2_ts_eos$spp_name %in% "B. alleghaniensis"]} RW/day; Figure \ref{fig:tsmuplotbspp}d and h; Table \ref{tab:ts_slope_table}), which also decreased growth with warmer thermal seasons. Estimates were unchanged when using the full or restricted datasets (SOS: $n =$
\Sexpr{nrow(empfullsosts)} and EOS: $n =$
\Sexpr{nrow(empfulleosts)} vs $n =$
\Sexpr{nrow(empts)}; Figure \ref{fig:tsSOSEOScomparison}b), or when using budburst instead of leafout as the start of the growing season (Figure \ref{fig:buburstVSleafoutTS}c). 

Finally, a later SOS delayed EOS only for {\Pdel} ($\beta$: 
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f",bspp_df2_ts_co$mean[bspp_df2_ts_co$spp_name %in% "P. deltoides"])}, UI:
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f",bspp_df2_ts_co$p5[bspp_df2_ts_co$spp_name %in% "P. deltoides"])},
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f",bspp_df2_ts_co$p95[bspp_df2_ts_co$spp_name %in% "P. deltoides"])} EOS/SOS; Figure \ref{fig:SOSonEOS}; Table \ref{tab:tab_SOSonEOS}). We did not find any clear difference in baseline growth between species (Figure \ref{fig:tsxyGDDyr}; \ref{fig:tsxyGSLyr}) and across year
s (Figure \ref{fig:tsmuplotayear}; Table \ref{tab:tab_all}), even in 2016, where there was an extreme drought ($\alpha$:
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f",ayear_df2_ts_gdd$mean[ayear_df2_ts_gdd$year_name %in% "2016"])}, UI:
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f",ayear_df2_ts_gdd$p5[ayear_df2_ts_gdd$year_name %in% "2016"])},
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f",ayear_df2_ts_gdd$p95[ayear_df2_ts_gdd$year_name %in% "2016"])} RW; Figure \ref{fig:tsmuplotayear}; Table \ref{tab:tab_all}). Growth was similar across individual trees (Figure \ref{fig:tsmuplotatreeid}).

% Figure mu across all species and climate predictors
\begin{figure}[!ht]
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{../../analyses/figures/growthModelsMain/TSmuALLbsppWlines.pdf}
\caption{Estimates of ring width change (log(mm)) as a function of four different phenological predictors: growing degree days of the growing season (mean daily temperature above 5\degree C); growing season length; start of season; end of season across 11 species: (Red maple (\textit{Acer rubrum}), Sugar maple (\textit{Acer saccharum}), Yellow buckeye (\textit{Aesculus flava}), Yellow birch (\textit{Betula alleghaniensis}), River birch (\textit{Betula nigra}), Pignut hickory (\textit{Carya glabra}), Shagbark hickory (\textit{Carya ovata}), Eastern cottonwood (\textit{Populus deltoides}), White oak (\textit{Quercus alba}), Northern red oak (\textit{Quercus rubra}) and American basswood (\textit{Tilia americana})); species colors are the same across the panels). In the legend, the growth speed of each species is depicted as slow (S), moderate (M) and fast (F) and ring-porous (R) and diffuse-porous (D) correspond to their wood anatomy (Table \ref{tab:tsCount}). Panels on the left (a to d) represent the mean model slope estimates for each species (dots) with 50\% and 90\% uncertainty intervals (thicker and thinner lines, respectively), for each predictor, which we represent through ring width change in mm on the log scale per adjusted predictor (see `Data analyses' section in Methods for more details on predictor adjustment). See Table \ref{tab:ts_slope_table} for parameter estimates. Panels on the right (e to h) show the ring width response curves as a function of each predictor, with the lines depicting the mean response at each predictor value, and the shaded area, the 50\% uncertainty intervals. These models do not include the effect of the previous year's GDD (see Figure \ref{fig:tsPrvsYr} for these estimates).}
\label{fig:tsmuplotbspp}
\end{figure}

